# 7 SQS priority queues - A
Q.append(dict(t="2.1", tp=["Amazon SQS", "Priority processing", "Amazon EC2 Auto Scaling"],
p="A company processes customer document-conversion jobs from a single Amazon SQS standard queue with a fleet of workers. Premium customers' jobs must start within 1 minute, but during busy periods they wait behind thousands of free-tier jobs. Free-tier jobs can wait several hours. The company wants premium jobs to be processed first without losing free-tier jobs. Which solution meets these requirements?",
o=["Send premium and free-tier jobs to two separate SQS queues, and have dedicated, separately scaled workers poll the premium queue so that it is always drained first.",
   "Set a message attribute named priority on each premium job so that SQS returns those messages before other messages in the queue.",
   "Convert the queue to an SQS FIFO queue and use the customer tier as the message group ID so that premium messages are delivered first.",
   "Set a message timer of 15 minutes on free-tier jobs so that they become visible only after the premium jobs have been processed."],
c="A",
w=[("Priority by queue", "SQS has no built-in message priority; using a separate queue for premium jobs isolates them from the free-tier backlog."),
   ("Independent scaling", "Workers dedicated to the premium queue (scaled on that queue's backlog) ensure premium jobs start quickly, while free-tier jobs are processed as capacity allows."),
   ("No loss", "Free-tier jobs remain in their own queue until processed.")],
x={"B": "SQS ignores message attributes when choosing which messages to return; there is no priority ordering.",
   "C": "Message group IDs preserve order within a group; they do not give one group priority over another.",
   "D": "A fixed delay of at most 15 minutes does not guarantee that premium jobs are processed first, and free-tier jobs would still block premium jobs after the delay."},
e=[("Priority with SQS", "Use separate queues per priority with dedicated or prioritized consumers."),
   ("Decoupling and scaling", "Scale each consumer group on its own queue's backlog per instance.")]))
# 8 KDS hot shard TWO (B, D)
Q.append(dict(t="2.1", tp=["Amazon Kinesis Data Streams", "Partition keys", "Resharding"],
p="An IoT company sends device readings to an Amazon Kinesis Data Streams stream in provisioned mode with 4 shards. The producers use the country code as the partition key, and 70% of devices are in one country. At peak, the aggregate write rate is 6 MB per second. Producers receive ProvisionedThroughputExceededException errors even though CloudWatch shows that three of the shards are lightly used. Ordering is required only per device. The company wants to stay on provisioned mode. Which combination of actions will resolve the errors? (Select TWO.)",
o=["Enable enhanced fan-out for all consumers of the stream.",
   "Change the partition key to the device ID so that records are distributed evenly across shards.",
   "Increase the data retention period of the stream from 24 hours to 7 days.",
   "Increase the number of shards to at least 8 by using the UpdateShardCount API.",
   "Increase the memory of the Lambda function that consumes the stream."],
c="BD",
w=[("High-cardinality key (B)", "Each shard accepts up to 1 MB/s or 1,000 records/s of writes. A low-cardinality key sends 70% of traffic to one shard (a hot shard). Device ID spreads writes evenly and still preserves per-device order."),
   ("Enough total capacity (D)", "6 MB/s exceeds the 4 MB/s total write capacity of 4 shards, so the stream needs more shards; 8 shards provide headroom."),
   ("Both are required", "Even distribution alone cannot exceed 4 MB/s, and more shards alone would still leave a hot shard.")],
x={"A": "Enhanced fan-out improves consumer read throughput; it does not affect producer write limits.",
   "C": "Retention does not change write throughput.",
   "E": "Consumer performance does not affect write throttling on producers."},
e=[("Hot shard symptoms", "Write throttling while other shards are idle -> choose a higher-cardinality partition key."),
   ("Shard math", "Write: 1 MB/s or 1,000 records/s per shard; read: 2 MB/s per shard (shared) or per consumer with enhanced fan-out.")]))
